% The search for the adornments a query reaches is charged every rule it
% reads against a configured rule limit, while the draft is charged only the
% rules it keeps.  p's rule is written four times and the draft keeps one
% copy, so the rewrite of this query generates three rules -- the seed
% recording q's binding, q$bf's rule and p$ff's copy of p's -- and would fit a
% limit of three; but the search reads q's rule and all four of p's, and
% refuses the query before it drafts anything.  Charging what the search
% reads is what bounds it when the rule limit is the only bound set.

e(a, b).
e(b, c).
f(a, x).

p(X, Y) :- e(X, Y).
p(X, Y) :- e(X, Y).
p(X, Y) :- e(X, Y).
p(X, Y) :- e(X, Y).
q(X, Y) :- f(X, Y).

p(X, Y), q(a, Z)?
